1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,733
Hi, and welcome to our full-stack AI portfolio
walkthrough.

2
00:00:05,733 --> 00:00:10,118
This project brings together a responsive portfolio
website and Deskwise,

3
00:00:10,118 --> 00:00:12,311
a working support assistant demo.

4
00:00:12,311 --> 00:00:15,412
The goal is to show a complete user journey,

5
00:00:15,412 --> 00:00:19,202
from a polished interface to a backend API and
persistent storage.

6
00:00:19,202 --> 00:00:21,632
The identity shown here is a placeholder,

7
00:00:21,632 --> 00:00:25,797
and the two additional project cards are clearly
marked as design concepts.

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,833
Let’s open the AI playground.

9
00:00:28,833 --> 00:00:32,315
Deskwise answers questions about a fictional store’s
returns,

10
00:00:32,315 --> 00:00:34,492
shipping, account access, and billing.

11
00:00:34,492 --> 00:00:37,493
For this recording, we’re using the built-in demo
mode.

12
00:00:37,493 --> 00:00:40,931
It retrieves sample policies and returns
deterministic responses,

13
00:00:40,931 --> 00:00:43,510
without making a live model request.

14
00:00:43,510 --> 00:00:46,168
When we ask how to return an order,

15
00:00:46,168 --> 00:00:49,490
the assistant displays the relevant policy and a
source reference.

16
00:00:49,490 --> 00:00:52,240
The conversation is saved through the Python
backend.

17
00:00:53,467 --> 00:00:56,141
Clicking the reference opens the knowledge base,

18
00:00:56,141 --> 00:00:59,580
so we can inspect the policy behind the response.

19
00:00:59,580 --> 00:01:04,989
This keeps the information visible instead of
presenting an answer without context.

20
00:01:04,989 --> 00:01:08,313
The project also includes an optional OpenAI
integration.

21
00:01:08,313 --> 00:01:11,969
With a valid API key configured securely on the
server,

22
00:01:11,969 --> 00:01:16,723
the backend sends selected policy excerpts and
recent conversation history to the model.

23
00:01:16,723 --> 00:01:20,553
That live integration is not being demonstrated in
this recording.

24
00:01:21,767 --> 00:01:24,560
Some questions need a human next step.

25
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:29,232
In the support tickets panel, we can create a ticket
for a delayed delivery,

26
00:01:29,232 --> 00:01:31,568
then mark it resolved or reopen it.

27
00:01:31,568 --> 00:01:36,043
These are local demo tickets, not messages sent to
an actual support team.

28
00:01:36,043 --> 00:01:39,978
Refreshing the page shows that the record persists.

29
00:01:39,978 --> 00:01:43,858
The database keeps each browser session’s
conversations and tickets separate,

30
00:01:43,858 --> 00:01:47,738
while the interface gives users clear feedback about
their actions.

31
00:01:48,967 --> 00:01:54,253
Under the hood, the application uses HTML, CSS, and
JavaScript on the frontend,

32
00:01:54,253 --> 00:01:58,320
Flask and Python for the API, and SQLite for
storage.

33
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:04,125
It includes input validation, CSRF checks, and
parameterized database queries.

34
00:02:04,125 --> 00:02:09,860
Six backend tests passed, along with browser checks
of the core workflow and mobile layout.

35
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:14,065
This is a portfolio demonstration, not a production
support platform.

36
00:02:14,065 --> 00:02:16,847
It provides a focused starting point for discussing

37
00:02:16,847 --> 00:02:19,629
your product requirements and building the next
iteration.

38
00:02:19,629 --> 00:02:21,080
Thanks for watching.
